\title{PAC+MQ is sometimes strictly stronger than EQMQ}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{#{X_n = \lbrace 0, 1\rbrace ^n}, #{\mathcal{C}_n = \lbrace \text{all Boolean functions in } n \text{ variables that take the value } 1 \text{ exactly once}\rbrace }.}

\p{\em{For PAC+MQ} (we don’t even need the MQ):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{1.} Take #{\approx \frac{1}{\varepsilon^2}} samples.}
\li{\strong{2.} If any sample has value #{1}, then output the function that only takes the value #{1} at that sample.}
\li{\strong{3.} If all samples are negative, return #{h \equiv 0}.}
}

\p{Notice if #{h \equiv 0},}

##{E_{\mathcal{D}}(h) = \mathbb{P}(h(x) \neq c(x)) = \mathbb{P}_{\mathcal{D}}(x_c) < \varepsilon,}

\p{where #{x_c} is the singular instance where #{c \neq 0}.}

\p{\em{For EQMQ:} this will fail because to hit an element of #{\mathcal{C}} on the nose, you must find a needle in a haystack (i.e. read through the entirety of #{X}).}
